WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.306
Dijkstra's algorithm finds the shortest distance from one start node

2
00:00:04.406 --> 00:00:07.832
to every other node. The numbers on the edges are lengths.

3
00:00:07.932 --> 00:00:09.773
Every length must be zero or more.

4
00:00:09.866 --> 00:00:12.164
At the start, A has distance zero.

5
00:00:12.264 --> 00:00:16.640
Every other node has distance infinity. Then repeat two steps.

6
00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:20.297
First, settle the unsettled node with the smallest distance.

7
00:00:20.397 --> 00:00:22.332
Second, relax its edges.

8
00:00:22.432 --> 00:00:26.095
To relax an edge, check whether the path through the settled node is

9
00:00:26.195 --> 00:00:26.586
shorter.

10
00:00:26.686 --> 00:00:30.384
If it is, keep the shorter distance, and remember where it came from.

11
00:00:30.483 --> 00:00:34.586
Settle A. From A, B is four away, and C is two away.

12
00:00:34.683 --> 00:00:38.526
The smallest estimate is now C, at two. Settle C.

13
00:00:38.626 --> 00:00:40.988
Through C, B is two plus one: three.

14
00:00:41.088 --> 00:00:43.834
That is shorter than four, so B becomes three.

15
00:00:43.934 --> 00:00:46.471
D becomes ten, and E becomes twelve.

16
00:00:46.566 --> 00:00:50.430
This is why the algorithm settles the smallest estimate, and not the

17
00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:51.808
first node it reached.

18
00:00:51.908 --> 00:00:55.758
Had it settled B at four, as soon as A reached it, the answer would be

19
00:00:55.858 --> 00:00:59.070
wrong. The path through C costs two plus one, which is three.

20
00:00:59.866 --> 00:01:02.856
Settle B, at three. It is now final.

21
00:01:02.956 --> 00:01:05.883
Every unsettled estimate is at least three:

22
00:01:05.983 --> 00:01:08.774
D is ten, E is twelve, and F is infinity.

23
00:01:08.874 --> 00:01:12.878
Any other path to B must pass one of them, and no length is negative.

24
00:01:12.978 --> 00:01:15.225
So no other path can cost less than three.

25
00:01:15.816 --> 00:01:20.470
Now relax B's edges. Through B, D is three plus five: eight.

26
00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:21.878
Eight is shorter than ten.

27
00:01:21.966 --> 00:01:26.304
Settle D, at eight. E becomes ten, and F becomes fourteen.

28
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.346
Pause here and predict. D is settled.

29
00:01:29.446 --> 00:01:31.970
Which node is settled next, and at what distance?

30
00:01:35.066 --> 00:01:38.888
E, at ten. It is the smallest unsettled estimate.

31
00:01:38.988 --> 00:01:40.765
Through E, F becomes thirteen.

32
00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:43.916
Settle F, at thirteen. Every node is settled.

33
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.620
Each node remembers the neighbor that gave its final distance.

34
00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:51.298
Follow them back from F: E, D, B, C, and then A.

35
00:01:51.398 --> 00:01:56.320
So the shortest path to F is A, C, B, D, E, F. Its length is thirteen.

36
00:01:57.917 --> 00:02:00.428
Why must every length be zero or more?

37
00:02:00.528 --> 00:02:04.844
Here is a small graph with one negative edge, from C to B, of length

38
00:02:04.944 --> 00:02:05.484
minus two.

39
00:02:05.583 --> 00:02:09.708
Settle A. From A, B is two away, and C is three away.

40
00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:13.890
B has the smallest estimate, so the algorithm settles B at two.

41
00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:15.042
It is now final.

42
00:02:15.133 --> 00:02:19.872
Then it settles C, at three. Through C, B costs three minus two: one.

43
00:02:19.972 --> 00:02:24.497
That is shorter, but B is already final. The algorithm answers two.

44
00:02:24.597 --> 00:02:26.012
The true distance is one.

45
00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:29.584
With negative lengths, use the Bellman-Ford algorithm instead.
